      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner now gives a clear position, relevant support from the novel, and explains why the circumstances invite sympathy. Confirm the response and provide a polished model answer, explaining that the memo accepts either position when it is substantiated. Since no mark total is supplied, do not invent a numerical allocation; describe the elements markers reward.",
      "content": "Your answer clearly states your view and supports it with relevant understanding of the novel. The grammar is also correct: “he remains.”\n\n**Model Answer:**\n> Yes, I have sympathy for him because he remains a man of faith and character despite the tragedies that befall his family and the loss of his son.\n\n### 📖 Concept & Thought Process (How to Understand It):\n* **The Concept / Rule:** This is an open-ended evaluative question. You may answer **yes** or **no**; the important part is to support your view with accurate, relevant evidence from the novel and explain how it supports your judgement.\n* **The Thought Process (How to Unpack It):**\n  1. Decide whether you sympathise with Stephen Kumalo.\n  2. Select a relevant detail from the novel, such as his distress over Absalom’s fate or the difficulties affecting his family.\n  3. Explain how that detail makes him deserving of sympathy, or why it does not, according to your view.\n* **How DBE Markers Score It:** The memo gives no fixed mark allocation here, so do not assume that simply writing “yes” or “no” earns credit. Markers look for a clear personal view supported by relevant understanding and an explanation. A well-substantiated “no” can also be accepted.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Avoid giving only a yes-or-no answer. Use a clear structure: **view + specific evidence + explanation**. Also check subject–verb agreement: write “he remains,” not “he remain.”"
    }
